def P(t): return "".join(f"<p>{x.strip()}</p>" for x in t.split("||"))
body = []
body.append('<h1>The Morning Letter</h1><div class="sub">With the compliments of the Editor, to Jonesboro, Brookland &amp; neighbors</div>')
body.append('<div class="date">Saturday, the Twenty-First of November, 2026</div><div class="orn">&#10086;</div>')
body.append('<p>Good morning,</p>')
body.append('<p>One more summery Saturday lies ahead, sunny and near 74, before Sunday’s clouds and rain bring a gray, chilly Thanksgiving week. Duck season opens this morning, and modern gun season for deer closes in Zone 4 tomorrow. Wall Street ended its worst week since the spring of 2025 with a steep Friday slide in technology shares; the region’s Fill the Food Bank drive sailed past its goal; and it is a full football Saturday, with Arkansas State playing its first game under an interim coach at Louisiana Tech, the Razorbacks at Texas and Tennessee at home against LSU.</p>')
body.append('<h2>The Day Ahead</h2>'); body.append(hourly); body.append(grid)
body.append(P('A cool, still start near 46 at six o’clock turns into one more borrowed day of summer: about 56 by nine, 71 at noon and a high near 74 by mid-afternoon under a cloudless sky, with only a light southerly breeze of 5 to 8 miles an hour and no rain to speak of. The evening stays mild, in the low 60s at suppertime and near 59 by nine.||Sunday turns gray and damp as a front arrives: clouds from the start, temperatures stuck in the low 60s, and a 64 percent chance of rain over the day, with the wind freshening toward 15 miles an hour by evening. Any rain will be welcome on parched ground. Behind the front, the week of Thanksgiving settles into proper late-autumn weather: 55 on Monday, the upper 40s Tuesday, and only the low to mid 40s Wednesday and Thanksgiving Day under mostly gray skies, with nights in the mid to upper 30s and little rain in sight after Sunday.'))
body.append('<h2>The Almanac</h2>')
body.append(f'''<table class="alm"><tr><td>First light</td><td>{alm['first_light']}</td><td>Sunrise</td><td>{alm['sunrise']}</td></tr>
<tr><td>Solar noon</td><td>{alm['noon']}</td><td>Sunset</td><td>{alm['sunset']}</td></tr>
<tr><td>Last light</td><td>{alm['last_light']}</td><td>Length of day</td><td>{alm['day_length']}</td></tr>
<tr><td>Change from yesterday</td><td>{alm['change'].replace('-','&minus;')}</td><td>Moon</td><td>{alm['moon']}, {alm['illum']}% lit</td></tr></table>''')
body.append(P('The sun rises at 6:45 and sets at 4:51, a day of ten hours and six minutes, nearly a minute and a half shorter than Friday’s. The moon, 82 percent lit and still waxing, is up in the afternoon and rides high through the evening; it rounds to full around Tuesday, the old Beaver Moon, so Thanksgiving week will have bright nights for anyone driving home late.||On this date in 1620, by the calendar we use today, the men aboard the Mayflower, anchored off Cape Cod, signed the Mayflower Compact, agreeing to govern themselves by common consent before going ashore. It was the same small company whose harvest meal the following autumn we remember next Thursday.'))
body.append(P('<i>In field and garden:</i> the pecans are dropping in earnest now, and it pays to gather them before the crows and squirrels do; spread them in a single layer somewhere dry for a couple of weeks to cure before cracking. Fallen leaves are worth more on the garden than at the curb: run the mower over them once and rake the shreds onto beds and around shrubs as a winter mulch. Turnip and mustard greens planted in the fall will sweeten after the colder nights coming this week. Along the Delta side of the county, look up on a still evening for the long, wavering lines of snow geese coming down for the winter, often heard before they are seen.'))
body.append('<p><i>Weather lore, not a forecast:</i> an old English saying runs, “If there’s ice in November that will bear a duck, there’ll be nothing after but sludge and muck,” meaning a hard early freeze promises a mild, messy winter. On the morning of duck season’s opener there is no ice in sight, and the old rhyme must wait.</p>')
body.append('<h2>Of Local Interest</h2>')
body.append("<h3>Fill the Food Bank tops its goal with 442,215 meals</h3>")
body.append(P("Northeast Arkansas gave the equivalent of 442,215 meals on Friday in the 19th annual Fill the Food Bank drive, comfortably past this year’s goal of 400,000. The one-day drive for the Food Bank of Northeast Arkansas, the Jonesboro-based charity that supplies pantries and meal programs across the region, ran from 6 a.m. to 6 p.m. at a record 17 drop-off sites in Jonesboro, Brookland, Paragould, Blytheville, Wynne, Walnut Ridge and elsewhere; what was given at the satellite sites stays in the county where it was given. Rebecca Hendrix, the food bank’s chief development officer, announced the total Friday evening.||The result matters because the shelves were thin going in: the food bank had less than 30 days of food on hand, its lowest level since 2019, as wholesale food costs rose 15 to 25 percent and year-round giving fell. This year’s total is below last year’s 535,473 meals. Each $1 provides three meals, and gifts are still accepted by texting NEAFOOD to 43414 or at foodbankofnea.org."))
body.append("<h3>Residents weigh in on Jonesboro’s 20-year growth plan</h3>")
body.append(P("Jonesboro residents got their first public look Thursday evening at the draft Comprehensive Growth Plan and Master Street Plan, the two halves of Plan Jonesboro, at a meeting at First Community Bank. The growth plan sets out where and how the city should expand over the next 20 years as it prepares for a population of 100,000; the street plan covers road connections, traffic flow and safety. The drafts went before the City Council on Tuesday.||Derrel Smith, the city’s director of planning and zoning, said the most common requests have been for more parks and green space, trails, safer streets and better traffic control, and that the city is taking the plans out to neighborhoods rather than waiting for people to come to City Hall, so that no one first sees them on adoption day. Julie Kelso, vice president of planning for the consulting firm Crafton Tull, said projects would be paid for from existing city money, grants and other sources, and that no new tax is being proposed.||The consultants are still revising the drafts, and both plans are expected to go to the council for adoption by the end of June 2027. The drafts and an online comment card are at PlanJonesboro.com."))
body.append("<h3>Judge presses both sides over money records in Lord’s Ranch abuse suits</h3>")
body.append(P("U.S. District Judge D.P. Marshall Jr. told lawyers at a hearing in Jonesboro this week that both sides were falling short of his expectations in a fight over financial records in the Lord’s Ranch lawsuits. Lord’s Ranch was a residential treatment center for children in Randolph County, taking in children sent by social service agencies from several states, that closed in 2016 after its owner, Ted Suhl, was convicted in a federal criminal case. Since 2023, former residents have filed federal suits alleging sexual abuse at the ranch going back to the 1980s, and seeking damages from Mr. Suhl, Shirley Suhl and two former employees. The defendants have denied the allegations, and none has been proved in court.||The plaintiffs want financial records from the Suhls and related businesses; the ranch’s operators received well over $200 million in Medicaid payments between 2001 and 2014. Defense lawyers said searches of the property and the Suhls’ homes turned up no records, and that Shirley Suhl, 90, testified that all ranch records were destroyed. They asked the judge to quash subpoenas to 17 financial institutions and 17 related entities. Judge Marshall urged the defense to “turn over more rocks” and asked whether Mr. Suhl would allow the IRS to release his records.||The case also got new life from a recent Arkansas Supreme Court ruling that such suits are not too late to file. Judge Marshall said he would set aside an earlier order that had dismissed some claims on those grounds, and the plaintiffs have asked him to reinstate 24 former residents whose claims were dropped last year. Trial is set for June 2028."))
body.append("<h3>From the courthouse</h3>")
body.append(P("Danny Lynn Millsap, 61, of Jonesboro, a Level 3 sex offender (the state’s designation for those judged at high risk of reoffending), now faces a Class C felony charge of possessing or viewing child sexual abuse material. Craighead County deputies arrested Mr. Millsap Sept. 23 on a charge of failing to register as a sex offender; a sheriff’s detective says his devices, and more found in a search of his home, held abuse images, including of very young children. Special Judge Barbara Halsey found probable cause Thursday and set bond at $500,000, with a GPS monitor and no electronic devices if he is released. The charge carries three to 10 years in prison.||Santana Carter is charged with aggravated assault and first-degree terroristic threatening, both felonies, accused of threatening to shoot a woman and then firing a shot inside a home on Caribbean Place on Thursday. No one was hurt; police say they found a casing, a bullet hole and a gun in the car. Bond is $50,000. Earlisha Watson, arrested early Thursday after a disturbance at a Phillips Drive motel, is charged with second-degree battery, a felony, accused of biting a Craighead County jailer on the forearm during booking; bond is $25,000. John Carl Bezek, 22, is charged with aggravated assault on a household member, accused of choking another man in a Nov. 11 apartment argument that police say began over cleaning; bond is $75,000.||Kimberly Marie Russell, 33, manager of the Vapor Maven vape shop at 2704 Alexander Drive, was arrested Wednesday after state regulators found kratom, a plant-based drug banned in Arkansas, displayed for sale; she is charged with possession with intent to deliver and was released on her own recognizance. And a woman cited for taking about $42 in household goods from an East Johnson Avenue store was charged with felony theft because of a 2022 theft conviction; her bond was set at $40,000. All are due in court Jan. 25. Charges are allegations, and all are presumed innocent.||Separately, the jury trial of James Austin Smith, 51, of Jonesboro, charged with second-degree sexual assault of a juvenile, has been moved from this week to Feb. 1 to 12, 2027. He remains held on $1 million bond."))
body.append("<h3>Police video shows dog bite after October chase</h3>")
body.append(P("Video released by the Jonesboro Police Department shows a police dog catching and biting Terry Lee Roedel, 48, on West Matthews Avenue on Oct. 18, after officers say he rode off on an electric bicycle when they tried to stop him on an outstanding felony warrant; the officer can be heard calling for medics, and Mr. Roedel was taken to a hospital. Police say they found about 51 grams of suspected methamphetamine and digital scales near his belongings. Drug task force agents arrested him Oct. 30, and he is charged with possession of methamphetamine with intent to deliver, possession of paraphernalia and tampering with evidence, all felonies, and misdemeanor fleeing. His bond is $100,000. The charges are allegations."))
body.append("<h3>A-State students collect for hungry pets</h3>")
body.append(P("Arkansas State University’s Pre-Veterinary Club, a student group for those headed to veterinary school, is collecting pet food and supplies through Friday, Dec. 4, for the Northeast Arkansas Humane Society and Jonesboro Animal Services, both of which help people who are struggling to feed their pets. Wanted are dog and cat food, toys, cat litter, bedding, paper and cleaning goods and laundry detergent. Drop-off boxes are at the A-State College of Veterinary Medicine, the second-floor information desk of the Reng Student Union, outside Room 227 of the College of Agriculture, the Lab Sciences East lobby, the Alpha Gamma Delta and Phi Delta Theta houses, and Hollywood Feed. Last year the club gathered more than 1,000 pounds."))
body.append("<h3>Around town</h3>")
body.append(P("<b>Saturday, Nov. 21.</b> Lit’l Bita Christmas, billed as the region’s largest arts and crafts show, runs 9 a.m. to 6 p.m. at First National Bank Arena, 217 Olympic Drive ($6, or $15 for all three days; photos with Santa 10 a.m. to noon and 1 to 5 p.m.). The Judd Hill Farmers Market on Aggie Road holds a winter market, 9 a.m. to 2 p.m. The Warrior Center gives away Thanksgiving turkeys from 11 a.m. while they last at Parker Park, 1506 N. Church St., one per family with a current utility bill; questions (870) 930-0187. “The Nutcracker” is at 7 p.m. at the Forum Theatre, 115 E. Monroe Ave. ($22 to $26). In Lepanto, NBA guard and hometown son Malik Monk’s foundation hands out turkey dinners to 450 families, 9 a.m. to 2 p.m., at First Missionary Baptist Church, 443 Oak St. Paragould High School’s “Leading Ladies” plays at 7 p.m., pay what you can.||<b>Sunday, Nov. 22.</b> Lit’l Bita Christmas, 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.; “The Nutcracker,” 2 p.m. NEA Baptist Memorial Hospital lights its Christmas tree at 5 p.m. in the lobby at 4800 E. Johnson Ave., with carols, cocoa, cookies and Santa; free.||<b>Monday, Nov. 23.</b> The Nashville family band Williamson Branch plays a holiday Bluegrass Monday at the Collins Theatre in Paragould at 7 p.m. ($10, 18 and under free). “The Nutcracker” also plays Monday and Tuesday at 7 p.m.||<b>Tuesday, Nov. 24.</b> Santa’s North Pole opens at 4502 Access Road ($8) and runs through Christmas Eve. The City Council’s Finance and Administration Committee meets at 4 p.m. It is the last day to register entries for the Downtown Paragould Christmas Parade.||<b>Thursday, Nov. 26, Thanksgiving.</b> The DAV Chapter 26 Turkey Trot 5K starts at 7 a.m. (registration 6 a.m.) at A-State’s military science building, 1921 Aggie Road; $40, benefiting free rides for veterans to VA appointments. The free Jonesboro Community Thanksgiving Meal is served 10 to 11:30 a.m., or until it runs out, at St. Bernards Auditorium, 505 E. Washington Ave.; delivery requires registration. Christmas at the Park opens at 5:30 p.m. at Joe Mack Campbell Park, 600 Harry Drive, with more than 400,000 lights, through Christmas Eve; $10 a vehicle on drive-through-only nights and $20 to $25 on others.||<b>Looking ahead.</b> The Downtown Paragould Christmas Parade, “Star Spangled Christmas,” steps off Tuesday, Dec. 1, at 7 p.m.; the Jonesboro Human Development Center holds its free Christmas parade Thursday, Dec. 3, at 1 p.m., 4701 Colony Drive."))
body.append("<h3>Our Neighbors Farther Afield</h3>")
body.append(P("<b>Osceola.</b> Main Street is now Odesther Young Lane. The City Council voted unanimously in October to honor Mrs. Young, who lived on the street for more than 30 years, looked after her neighbors and died in August at 100, and family and friends unveiled the new sign with a balloon release Friday. “She was literally the community’s MaDea,” said her grandson, A.J. Young.||<b>Batesville.</b> Joshua Joel Adame, 40, who ran from the Independence County jail Thursday afternoon, was back in custody shortly after 6:30 a.m. Friday. Acting on a tip, Batesville police found him inside an abandoned house on North Central Avenue and arrested him without incident."))
body.append('<div style="break-inside:avoid;page-break-inside:avoid"><h2>From the Police Reports</h2><p class="note">Jonesboro Police incident reports. Charges are allegations. No names are printed.</p><p>The latest batch covers 37 reports filed from Thursday morning to early Friday, with 7 arrests and one citation.</p></div>')
body.append('<p class="note" style="break-after:avoid;margin-bottom:2pt"><b>Overnight and early Friday</b></p>')
body.append('<p><b>Shot fired during a dispute on Caribbean Place.</b> Officers sent to a disturbance on Caribbean Place in the 72404 area at about 11:40 p.m. Thursday arrested a woman shortly before 1 a.m. on felony charges of aggravated assault and first-degree terroristic threatening. She is accused of threatening an acquaintance and pulling a handgun. A city charge of firing a gun inside the city limits was also filed, and a revolver and a spent casing were taken as evidence. The report lists no injuries.</p>')
body.append('<p><b>Same Mabrey Lane property targeted three times.</b> Two backpack leaf blowers and a gas can were taken from a property on Mabrey Lane Thursday morning and later turned up in the woods behind it. That evening neighbors held a man there until police arrived, and he was arrested on charges of criminal trespass and possessing an instrument of crime, a breaker bar. Then at about 4:50 a.m. Friday a camera caught someone trying to hot-wire a four-wheeler at the same place, reported as an attempted felony theft. Police have grouped these with earlier break-in reports on Mabrey Lane and Charles Drive.</p>')
body.append('<p><b>Car prowlers before dawn.</b> At about 5:20 a.m. Friday two people were reported pulling on car door handles on Lawrence Street, a felony breaking-or-entering report. Nearby on Mays Road, someone got into a car parked in a driveway and took a new tire. Police are treating the two cases as linked; no arrests yet.</p>')
body.append('<p><b>Five hit-and-runs.</b> A dark SUV, possibly a Dodge Durango, sideswiped a car heading east on East Johnson Avenue near University Loop East at about 6 a.m. Friday. Late Thursday a dark pickup with a camper shell hit a vehicle on North Airport Road and kept going. Each of those two left an estimated $1,000 to $10,000 in damage. Smaller hit-and-runs were reported in a parking lot on North Church Street Thursday evening and near Race Street late Thursday morning, where a man said a white truck struck him. A fifth report covered a car damaged Oct. 4 on East Johnson Avenue.</p>')
body.append('<p class="note" style="break-after:avoid;margin-bottom:2pt"><b>Thursday</b></p>')
body.append('<p><b>Rifle stolen from a police SUV.</b> Someone broke a rear window of a Jonesboro officer’s city-issued Ford Explorer overnight Wednesday into Thursday and took a patrol rifle with its sight, a weapon light, magazines and ammunition, worth about $2,000 in all. It is filed as felony theft of a firearm and breaking or entering. The report does not give the location.</p>')
body.append('<p><b>Stolen car and vandalism.</b> A black 2021 Nissan Altima was stolen overnight on North Bridge Street, a felony vehicle-theft report. A rock broke the front window of a salon on Marketplace Drive, about $1,000 in damage. During a prowler call Thursday evening on Ocean Drive, someone tore a video doorbell off a house. A tire was also let down on an SUV on East Nettleton Avenue.</p>')
body.append('<p><b>Scams and threats.</b> A used-car lot on East Johnson Avenue reported a fake website selling cars under its name, filed as felony identity fraud. A woman on Union Street reported a stream of harassing messages from a man she knows. On Walnut Street, a man is accused of slapping another man and threatening him. That report lists a felony first-degree terroristic threatening charge and a second-degree assault charge, but no arrest yet. An attempted sexual-extortion report was also taken.</p>')
body.append('<p><b>Warrants and traffic stops.</b> A woman was arrested Thursday afternoon on a felony bench warrant on Griffin Street. A man seen walking in the road near East Word Avenue and North Allis Street was arrested on a probation-revocation warrant. A driver stopped at East Johnson Avenue and North Caraway Road was arrested on a failure-to-appear warrant. A woman stopped at Bradley and French streets was cited for driving without a license and on an unpaid-fines warrant.</p>')
body.append('<p><b>Also reported.</b> A third-degree domestic assault report, with one arrest, 72401 area; a third-degree domestic battery report, 72405 area; a school incident report, juvenile suspect, 72401 area; and a second-degree terroristic threatening report, juvenile victim, 72404 area.</p>')
body.append('<p><i>A word to the wise:</i> Overnight prowlers are going down driveways trying door handles. Lock your car even in your own driveway, and bring guns, tools and anything of value inside at night. If you see someone checking cars, call 911 rather than confronting them, and save any doorbell or camera video for the officer.</p>')
body.append('<div><p class="note" style="break-after:avoid">At the county jails</p><p>The Craighead County jail roster listed 312 people held Saturday morning, with 30 booked in the last 48 hours. Among those listed first, the most common entries were holds for other agencies and failure-to-appear warrants, along with public intoxication and aggravated assault on a family or household member. The highest bond shown was $75,000, on an aggravated-assault-on-a-family-member charge. In Randolph County, the sheriff’s roster listed 61 people held. One person was booked since Thursday, on a release-conditions violation, two were released in the last 48 hours, and no bond was listed for the newest booking. Charges are allegations, and being on a roster does not mean anyone has been convicted.</p></div>')
body.append('<h2>From the State</h2>')
body.append('<h3>Five probable measles cases reported in Lawrence County, the state’s first this year</h3>')
body.append(P('The Arkansas Department of Health announced Friday that it has identified five probable measles cases in Lawrence County, in northeast Arkansas. They are the first cases in the state this year. All five people are unvaccinated residents who appear to have caught the virus while traveling in another state with a current outbreak; the department did not say which state. The cases are called “probable” because they rest on symptoms and exposure history and have not yet been confirmed by laboratory tests. No hospitalizations were reported. || Measles is a highly contagious viral illness that begins with fever, cough, runny nose and red, watery eyes, followed a few days later by a rash; a person can spread it from four days before to four days after the rash appears. State Epidemiologist Dr. Austin Porter said there is no evidence yet of spread within Arkansas and no other known cases in the state, so officials are not calling it an outbreak. Dr. Meghan Repp, president of the Arkansas chapter of the American Academy of Pediatrics, said community spread is “very likely” given the state’s low vaccination rates. || Only about 90 percent of Arkansas toddlers have had the measles, mumps and rubella (MMR) shot, among the lowest rates in the country and below the roughly 95 percent needed to keep the disease from spreading; Lawrence County is about the same as the state. Two doses give long-lasting protection, and the department says a dose given soon after exposure can stop the infection. People born before 1957 are generally considered immune. The United States has recorded 5,838 cases this year, Missouri 25 and Tennessee 11, and the country lost its measles-elimination status this week.'))
body.append('<h3>Federal judge refuses to approve deal freeing $6 billion data center from Pulaski County rules</h3>')
body.append(P('U.S. District Judge Lee Rudofsky on Friday declined to sign a proposed settlement between Pulaski County and AVAIO Digital Partners, the developer of a planned $6 billion data center outside Wrightsville, south of Little Rock. Data centers are large buildings full of computer servers, of the kind that power artificial intelligence, and they use great amounts of electricity and water. AVAIO sued in October over a county ordinance regulating such projects, which the Quorum Court (the county’s governing body of elected justices of the peace) passed in the fall and then enacted over County Judge Barry Hyde’s veto by a 10-4 vote. || The deal, worked out between AVAIO and Mr. Hyde, who leaves office in about six weeks after losing his primary, would have declared that the ordinance does not apply to the project, because its site plan was approved before the rules took effect. In return AVAIO would have dropped its claims and its demand for legal fees. The judge said the agreement “all looks a little too perfect” and that it “leaves a very bad taste in my mouth,” in part because it could bind future county officials. Justice of the Peace Julie Blackwood, whom AVAIO accuses of withholding public records, objected through her lawyer. || Wendell Griffen, a former circuit judge who becomes county judge Jan. 1 and will have to enforce the ordinance, said he was “pleased” the deal was rejected; Judge Rudofsky had earlier denied his request to join the case. The hearing on AVAIO’s request for an order blocking the ordinance resumes at 8:30 a.m. Monday.'))
body.append('<h3>Arkansas jobless rate dips to 3.8 percent as payrolls set a record</h3>')
body.append(P('Arkansas’ unemployment rate fell to 3.8 percent in October from 3.9 percent in September, the state Department of Commerce reported Friday, below the national rate of 4.1 percent. The number of nonfarm payroll jobs, a count of positions at businesses and government offices, rose by 8,700 in the month to a record, and both of the government’s job surveys reached all-time highs. The share of adults working or looking for work rose to 59.2 percent. || Over the past year, leisure and hospitality added 4,300 jobs, the most of any sector; trade, transportation and utilities added 2,600; and construction added 2,000, reaching a record. Financial services were the only sector to lose jobs. Michael Pakko, chief economist at the Arkansas Economic Development Institute at the University of Arkansas at Little Rock, said “labor market conditions in Arkansas have strengthened over the course of 2026.” The figures are preliminary and may be revised; some year-over-year comparisons are unavailable because last year’s federal shutdown halted the October 2025 survey.'))
body.append('<h3>Texas water firm offers Texarkana up to $1.65 million as Millwood Lake sale advances</h3>')
body.append(P('Heartland Water Partners, a Texas company, is offering Texarkana, Ark., up to $1.65 million in three installments of $550,000 as it pursues a plan to pipe water from Millwood Lake, a reservoir on the Little River in southwest Arkansas, about 127 miles to Bois d’Arc Lake in North Texas for the North Texas Municipal Water District. The payments would come as the project clears three steps: a state application, approval by the Legislature and a signed final contract. The towns of Ashdown and Ogden are offered the same terms. || Texarkana could commit up to 110 million gallons a day. The preliminary price is 45 to 55 cents per 1,000 gallons, which City Manager Tyler Richards estimated could bring the city up to $20 million a year, rising toward $50 million by the end of the proposed 40-year contract; he cautioned that “the rate is not final.” The cities could not entertain other offers for that water through 2027. || The sale cannot go forward without the General Assembly’s approval. The Arkansas Natural Resources Commission, the state body that manages water resources, will hold a public hearing in southwest Arkansas next month and then make a recommendation to lawmakers, and each city council must also approve a final contract. The proposal comes as more than half the state sits in exceptional drought.'))
body.append('<h3>Parole board vacancy filled after nine months</h3>')
body.append(P('Gov. Sarah Huckabee Sanders on Friday appointed Kevin Sontag, a former Arkansas State Police forensic scientist, to the Post-Prison Transfer Board, the seven-member panel that decides parole and makes recommendations on pardons and sentence commutations. The seat had been empty since John Felts, a member since 1998, left at the end of January. Because granting parole takes five votes no matter how many members are seated, the vacancy made approvals harder; the board’s chairman, Boyce Hamlet, told lawmakers this month the panel had been short a member for almost the entire year. || Mr. Sontag, a Sherwood native and Army veteran, worked from 2009 to 2020 as a senior forensic scientist with the State Police on more than 1,800 criminal cases, according to the governor’s office, and most recently was an assistant division chief at the Arkansas Game and Fish Commission. The full-time post pays $127,600, and his term runs to January 2032. The governor also named several state district judges on Friday.'))
body.append('<h3>In brief</h3>')
body.append(P('<b>Ethics Commission names director.</b> The Arkansas Ethics Commission, which enforces the state’s campaign-finance, lobbying and disclosure laws, named Jill Barham its executive director after 11 months as interim. A staff attorney there since 2012 and a former deputy prosecutor, she succeeds Graham Sloan, who retired Dec. 31 after leading the agency since 2000. || <b>North Little Rock officer fired over plate-camera search.</b> North Little Rock police fired Sgt. Jhailan Rathey on Thursday after an internal investigation found he used the department’s Flock Safety license-plate reader system, a network of cameras that log passing vehicles, for “a personal matter.” He is the second Arkansas officer reported for misusing the system in recent months, and the department now requires monthly audits of searches. || <b>Pearl Harbor sailor identified.</b> Navy Seaman 2nd Class Kenneth Lee Lynch, a native of southwest Arkansas killed aboard the battleship USS Utah in the Dec. 7, 1941, attack on Pearl Harbor, has been identified through DNA from a cousin, almost 85 years later. His remains will be buried in Denton, Texas, where his family later moved, after they arrive Dec. 4.'))
body.append('<h2>From the Nation</h2>')
body.append('<h3>Trump hails Asia trip, threatens tax on data centers built abroad</h3>')
body.append(P('President Trump, back from a week-long trip to Asia, held a news conference of nearly two hours at the White House on Friday, joined by Vice President JD Vance, Secretary of State Marco Rubio and his new chief of staff, Stephen Miller. He called the journey “one of the most successful presidential visits to Asia in American history,” citing more than $17 billion in commercial deals, and said a trade agreement with Mexico was “getting close.” || Mr. Trump defended his friendly relations with China’s leader, Xi Jinping, and Russia’s president, Vladimir Putin, whom he met together in Shenzhen, China, on Thursday, calling critics of those ties “stupid.” He said Mr. Putin “clearly expressed” a wish to end the war in Ukraine by diplomacy, though Russia launched new strikes on Ukraine after the meeting. He said he and Mr. Xi had agreed on “joint guardrails” for artificial intelligence but gave no details. || He also said American technology companies that build AI data centers in other countries would face “a tremendous tax,” adding, “We want them to build it here.” He gave no details and said he had not yet told his own officials about the idea. He said he would like to see Jensen Huang, the Nvidia co-founder who resigned as chief executive this month, “make a big apology and go back”; Nvidia has said Mr. Huang will not return. || Asked about news reports that Iran was planning a possible “dirty bomb” attack (a conventional explosive that spreads radioactive material) on officials in Washington, he called it a “rumor” but warned, “If they do, we’re going to fight.” Mr. Rubio has said the country faces no “imminent threat,” and Iran has called the reports baseless. Mr. Trump again described this week’s string of outages at banks, airlines and government agencies as “all separate events,” and said he pushed out the national cyber director because “I didn’t think that person was good enough.”'))
body.append('<h3>SEC reportedly examining Nvidia’s accounting as stocks finish their worst week in years</h3>')
body.append(P('Staff at the Securities and Exchange Commission, the federal regulator that polices the stock market, are examining possible accounting problems at Nvidia, the Santa Clara, Calif., company whose chips power most artificial-intelligence systems, according to news reports Friday citing two people briefed on the matter. An SEC investigation into whether the company’s disclosures misled investors had been reported earlier. It is not known when the inquiry began or exactly what it covers. The SEC declined to comment and Nvidia did not respond; an investigation does not mean wrongdoing has been found. || Nvidia shares fell 14 percent Friday to $125.53 and 23 percent for the week, their worst week since 2008, leaving the stock about 48 percent below its October record. The company has been in turmoil since Mr. Huang resigned Nov. 12, and California’s attorney general subpoenaed it this week over whether its chips reached China in violation of export rules; its chief financial officer, Colette Kress, is interim chief executive. || The selloff helped push the Standard &amp; Poor’s 500 index into what Wall Street calls a correction, a drop of 10 percent from its peak.'))
body.append('<h3>Hackers breach Agriculture Department farm-payment system and FBI crime database</h3>')
body.append(P('Hackers broke into the U.S. Department of Agriculture’s system for paying farmers, the department told employees Thursday, according to news reports Friday citing people familiar with the matter. Separately, the FBI is investigating an apparent breach of the National Crime Information Center, the nationwide database that federal, state and local police use to check warrants, stolen property and criminal records, according to people briefed on it. || Officials have not said who is responsible, what information was taken, or whether payments to farmers have been delayed. Officials said that as of Friday no other USDA systems had been hit, but former department officials warned the payment system could serve as a gateway to more sensitive records, including food-safety data. The FBI had no comment, and it is not known whether the crime database’s records were altered. It is the bureau’s second known breach since September, when a hacking group claimed to have stolen employee data from its job-application website. || The breaches follow a week of unrelated failures at airlines, banks and the National Security Agency that officials say were not attacks; the NSA restored a classified network this week after a three-day outage it blamed on a maintenance problem.'))
body.append('<h3>Federal appeals court halts Army’s planned explosive blast in Iowa</h3>')
body.append(P('The U.S. Court of Appeals for the 8th Circuit, based in St. Louis, has temporarily blocked the Army from blowing up nearly five tons of degraded lead azide, a highly sensitive explosive used to set off larger charges, at the Iowa Army Ammunition Plant in Middletown, in southeast Iowa near the Mississippi River. The Army had already postponed the detonation from Friday to Monday. The court’s order, issued late Thursday, pauses the Environmental Protection Agency’s emergency permit for the blast while the judges consider a longer halt. || The Iowa Environmental Council and the Sierra Club’s Iowa chapter asked for the stay, arguing that the EPA approved the plan without weighing its health effects or alternatives and that lead dust, a toxin especially harmful to children, could drift over an area about 25 miles long and 5 miles wide, including homes and waterways. The Justice Department said it would file its opposition by Sunday. || The Army, the EPA and the Department of Health and Human Services said Friday that the material is too unstable to move and that destroying it in place is the only responsible option, with “extreme mitigation measures.” Acting Army Secretary Adam Telle has said Monday was the latest safe date before a forecast cold snap that could make the material more dangerous. No date has been set for the court’s decision.'))
body.append('<h3>Senate rejects bid to make Trump end the Iran war or seek Congress’ approval</h3>')
body.append(P('The Senate on Thursday voted 51 to 48 against a resolution that would have directed President Trump to remove U.S. forces from hostilities with Iran unless Congress authorized the war. The measure was brought under the War Powers Resolution, a 1973 law meant to limit a president’s ability to keep troops in combat without Congress’ approval; the Constitution gives Congress the power to declare war, and Mr. Trump never sought authorization. It was the first such vote since the Nov. 3 midterm elections. || Three Republicans, Sens. Susan Collins of Maine, Lisa Murkowski of Alaska and Rand Paul of Kentucky, voted with Democrats in favor, and Sen. John Fetterman of Pennsylvania was the only Democrat opposed. Sen. Tim Kaine, a Virginia Democrat and a longtime advocate for Congress’ war powers, said the war’s unpopularity “was made absolutely, manifestly clear in the election results.” Sen. Thom Tillis, a North Carolina Republican who backed a similar measure in June, voted no, saying, “This one doesn’t go to the president, so it’s just a statement from Congress.” || The House passed the resolution 220 to 204 in September. Democrats take control of the Senate on Jan. 3 and have pledged to make ending the war a priority. Before then, Congress must pass a spending bill by Dec. 11 to avoid a partial government shutdown.'))
body.append('<h3>Penny formally retired as Trump signs law allowing cash totals to be rounded</h3>')
body.append(P('President Trump on Friday signed the Common Cents Act, a bipartisan law that formally ends the minting of pennies for circulation and lets stores round cash purchases to the nearest 5 cents. The U.S. Mint stopped making pennies in November 2025, after Mr. Trump called for an end to production, but until now merchants had no federal rules for making change without them. || Under the law, a cash total of $10.02 would round down to $10, and $10.04 would round up to $10.05. Card and other electronic payments will still be charged to the exact cent. Pennies remain legal tender, and the Treasury says the Federal Reserve will keep recirculating the roughly 114 billion still in use for as long as possible. The law also allows the nickel to be made with a cheaper zinc core. || The bill’s author, Rep. Lisa McClain, a Michigan Republican, said each penny costs nearly four cents to make and projected savings of $85 million a year; the Mint has estimated $56 million. It passed both chambers by voice vote. Retailers welcomed it: the National Association of Convenience Stores said stores had sought federal guidance on cash transactions since production ended.'))
body.append('<h3>In brief</h3>')
body.append(P('<b>Coastal storm threatens Thanksgiving travel.</b> A storm off Florida’s east coast, which the National Hurricane Center says could become Subtropical or Tropical Storm Kyle, is expected to bring heavy rain, gusty winds, rough surf and coastal flooding from northeast Florida through Georgia and the Carolinas today, then to the Mid-Atlantic and New England Sunday and Monday, with possible delays for early holiday travelers. || <b>Judge limits agriculture secretary’s religious emails.</b> U.S. District Judge Trina Thompson in San Francisco ruled Thursday that Agriculture Secretary Brooke Rollins likely violated the First Amendment’s ban on government establishment of religion with Christian holiday messages sent to USDA employees from her official account, and issued a preliminary injunction against such emails. A USDA spokesperson called it “a completely unprecedented attempt to muzzle a Cabinet Secretary,” and the department is weighing an appeal. || <b>Refugee policies in court.</b> A federal judge in Boston, Myong Joun, appeared inclined Friday to block an administration freeze on refugees’ green-card applications and a rule cutting from 30 days to 10 the time refugees have to contest the end of their legal status; he has not ruled. || <b>Former Jackson mayor sentenced.</b> Chokwe Antar Lumumba, former mayor of Jackson, Miss., was sentenced Friday to 37 months in federal prison for a bribery conspiracy in which he took $50,000 in campaign money from undercover FBI agents posing as hotel developers.'))
body.append('<h2>From Abroad</h2>')
body.append('<h3>Ukraine counts its dead as Poland asks NATO for more air defense</h3>')
body.append(P('The death toll from Russia’s overnight air attack on Ukraine into Friday, one of the largest of the war, rose to eight, Ukrainian officials said: six in Kyiv, including three workers at a water facility, and two in the western city of Lviv, with 28 people wounded. President Volodymyr Zelenskyy said Russia fired more than 400 drones and more than 110 missiles, 27 of them ballistic, at energy, transport and rail sites. Ukraine’s nuclear power plants had to cut their output, and emergency power cuts hit Kyiv and 10 regions, according to First Deputy Energy Minister Artem Nekrasov. Russia’s Defense Ministry said it struck Kyiv’s road and rail bridges because they carry military equipment, and the attack followed President Vladimir Putin’s vow to answer Ukraine’s strikes on the Kerch Bridge to occupied Crimea. || In Poland, a NATO member, where two Russian missiles and a jet-powered drone are believed to have come down in the southeast during the attack, Defense Minister Władysław Kosiniak-Kamysz called it “another provocation” and said Warsaw was formally asking NATO for more forces under Operation Eastern Sentry, the alliance’s air-defense mission on its eastern edge. Prime Minister Donald Tusk said “nobody can say that this was an innocent coincidence.” Some early reports said Poland had invoked Article 4 of the NATO treaty, which calls for formal consultations among allies; it had not done so as of midday Saturday in Europe. NATO Secretary General Mark Rutte, who called the incident “clearly dangerous,” was due in Warsaw on Saturday for talks with the Polish government. || In Washington, President Trump told a nearly two-hour White House news conference Friday that he and Mr. Putin had “very good talks” in China and that there is a “real good chance” of a peace deal, but he declined to discuss security guarantees for Ukraine, saying “we got to get a deal first.” His envoy Jared Kushner had said Russia was comfortable with NATO-style guarantees for postwar Ukraine, while Russian Deputy Foreign Minister Sergei Ryabkov said any form of NATO “quasi membership” for Kyiv is unacceptable; Mr. Trump called the mixed signals negotiating tactics.'))
body.append('<h3>Iran says it shot down two U.S. drones as Washington adds sanctions</h3>')
body.append(P('Iran’s Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps, the elite branch of its armed forces, said Saturday that a new air-defense system shot down an American MQ-9 Reaper drone over the Strait of Hormuz, the narrow Gulf waterway that carried about a fifth of the world’s oil and gas in peacetime, and later said it downed a smaller MQ-1 drone over Iran’s Qeshm Island in the strait. The claims were carried by Iranian state broadcasting, and the U.S. military did not immediately comment. The U.S. and Israel went to war with Iran on February 28; a June agreement between Washington and Tehran was never fully carried out, and attacks have flared periodically since July, including recent tit-for-tat strikes and Iranian attacks on ships. || On Friday the U.S. Treasury sanctioned four Iranians it called regime “oil trustees,” who it says collect money from illicit oil sales abroad to get around sanctions and the U.S. naval blockade of Iran’s southern ports, and who often kept the money for themselves. They include a former Revolutionary Guard intelligence officer, the brother of Iran’s football federation president and the sons of a Revolutionary Guard general and of the head of Mahan Air, an Iranian airline. || Iran’s foreign minister, Abbas Araghchi, told state media Friday that the strait will not reopen until seven Iranian conditions are met, including an end to the war and the lifting of sanctions and the blockade, and he again ruled out shipping Iran’s highly enriched uranium abroad. Mr. Trump said at his news conference that Iran wants to make a deal, but not one he can accept, adding that the U.S. wants “a real deal, not a fake deal.”'))
body.append('<h3>Climate summit runs into overtime over fossil fuels</h3>')
body.append(P('The United Nations climate summit known as COP31, in Antalya, Turkey, missed its Friday deadline with nearly 200 countries still deadlocked, and negotiators worked through the night toward a third draft of the final agreement promised for Saturday morning. The fight is over the latest draft, which dropped every mention of fossil fuels after objections from Saudi Arabia and other oil and gas producers, and which also cut a proposed $120 billion target for helping poorer countries adapt to climate change. || A group of about a dozen European and developing countries, including France and Panama, signed a letter demanding “a clear transition away from fossil fuels.” Panama’s environment minister, Juan Carlos Navarro, blamed a “petroleum mafia” and threatened to walk out or formally object, and the European Union warned the text “risks turning this climate COP into a fossil summit.” || The summit’s president, Turkish Environment Minister Murat Kurum, said, “We are close to the finish line, but we are not there yet,” and Australia’s climate minister, Chris Bowen, who is leading the negotiations, said he would work “for as long as it takes.” Expectations for big changes are low. The United States is not taking part for a second year. On the sidelines, host Turkey says it has signed deals and plans worth more than $26 billion during the conference, including a preliminary $15 billion renewable-energy agreement with China.'))
body.append('<h3>China tells Japan to “correct” its Taiwan stance after a summit snub</h3>')
body.append(P('China’s Foreign Ministry said Friday that Japan’s top priority must be to “correct its wrong words and actions on the Taiwan issue as soon as possible,” a day after Chinese President Xi Jinping declined to hold a formal meeting with Japanese Prime Minister Sanae Takaichi at the Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation summit in Shenzhen, China. The two leaders shook hands twice but did not sit down for talks. || The quarrel dates to about a year ago, when Ms. Takaichi, a conservative in Japan’s ruling party, suggested Japan might respond militarily if China tried to take Taiwan, the self-governed island Beijing claims, by force. China answered with trade restrictions and a warning to its citizens not to travel to Japan. Ms. Takaichi said Japan’s position is unchanged and that it still follows the 1972 agreement that restored ties with Beijing, adding that because the two countries have unresolved issues, “it is important to engage in dialogue.” || At the same summit she held her first face-to-face talks with Mr. Putin, at a time when relations between Tokyo and Moscow are also strained.'))
body.append('<h3>Saudi-led coalition launches a new air campaign in Yemen</h3>')
body.append(P('The Saudi-led military coalition fighting Yemen’s Houthi rebels, the Iran-aligned movement that holds the capital, Sanaa, announced a new operation Friday that it said targets the sources of Houthi missile and drone attacks on Saudi Arabia. The coalition’s spokesman, Maj. Gen. Turki al-Maliki, said strikes hit a main Houthi supply depot and missile and drone workshops in Sanaa and weapons facilities at the Red Sea port of Hodeidah, whose workers were told to leave beforehand, and that strikes were continuing in Saada and Taiz provinces. There was no immediate Houthi response. (single source: Middle East Monitor) || The campaign comes as the Houthis push toward Aden, the southern port city that serves as the seat of Yemen’s internationally recognized government. Government forces pulled back from the Red Sea coast near the Bab el-Mandeb strait last weekend to defend Aden, and the Houthis have fired missiles at Riyadh and Saudi oil sites. Yemen’s civil war had been mostly quiet since 2022 but reignited this summer as a spillover of the Iran war; the United Nations says nearly 357,000 people have been displaced since August.'))
body.append('<h3>Young people march across Europe over living costs and military spending</h3>')
body.append(P('Tens of thousands of students and young workers took to the streets Friday in a coordinated day of action in more than 25 European countries, protesting the rising cost of living, tuition fees, cuts to public services and growing military budgets. In Italy, where unions called a national strike and train disruptions were planned through Saturday, protesters filled more than 100 town squares, including Milan’s Piazza del Duomo; Maurizio Landini, head of the CGIL, Italy’s largest labor union, claimed more than a million took part, a figure not independently confirmed. || In Britain, students marched toward Parliament Square in London, and organizers said more than 350 schools, colleges and universities planned walkouts; there were also rallies in Germany, France and Spain. Demonstrators also opposed military conscription and what they called European governments’ complicity in Israel’s war in Gaza. “Fight for our future, not their wars,” said Adam Powell-Davies, national chair of the group Socialist Students. (single source: CNN)'))
body.append('<h3>In brief</h3>')
body.append(P('<b>Gaza drone strikes.</b> Israeli drone strikes killed at least seven Palestinians Friday in central Gaza, four of them in the Bureij refugee camp and others in Nuseirat and at the entrance to Deir el-Balah, according to local medical sources; the Israeli military did not comment on the strikes. The deaths came days after Mr. Trump’s Board of Peace, the international body overseeing the Gaza truce of October 2025, warned the U.N. Security Council that the ceasefire could collapse without immediate action and faulted both Israel and Hamas. || <b>Sudan drone strikes.</b> Amnesty International, the human-rights group, urged that drone strikes by the Rapid Support Forces, the paramilitary fighting Sudan’s army in a civil war since 2023, be investigated as war crimes; it documented nine strikes in and around El Obeid in North Kordofan between June and August that killed at least 37 civilians. The RSF said it was considering setting up its own investigative committee.'))
body.append('<div style="break-inside:avoid;page-break-inside:avoid"><h2>Markets &amp; the Farm</h2><p class="note">Figures are as of Friday’s close, November 20. Week changes are measured from the close of Friday, November 13.</p><table class="mk"><tr><th>Index</th><th>Close</th><th>Day</th><th>Week</th></tr><tr><td>S&amp;P 500</td><td class="n">6,910.39</td><td class="n">−265.44 (−3.7%)</td><td class="n">−651.28 (−8.6%)</td></tr><tr><td>Dow Jones Industrials</td><td class="n">48,147.33</td><td class="n">−949.73 (−1.9%)</td><td class="n">−4,200.22 (−8.0%)</td></tr><tr><td>Nasdaq Composite</td><td class="n">22,617.73</td><td class="n">−1,261.47 (−5.3%)</td><td class="n">−2,518.54 (−10.0%)</td></tr></table><p>The selling got worse on Friday. A rout in artificial-intelligence and computer-chip stocks pushed the S&amp;P 500 down for a seventh straight day, and both the S&amp;P 500 and the Dow closed in a “correction,” Wall Street’s term for a drop of 10 percent or more from a record high. Nvidia, the leading maker of AI chips, fell 14 percent, and Broadcom, AMD and Micron each lost about 13 to 14 percent, as investors questioned whether the huge sums spent on AI data centers will pay off. The tech-heavy Nasdaq wiped out its gain for the year, Anthropic, the AI company that began trading Thursday, gave back 8.5 percent, and the S&amp;P 500 had its worst week since April 2025. Money moved into U.S. Treasury bonds, and a closely watched gauge of investor fear, the VIX, rose above 30 for the first time since March.</p><p>Next week is short: markets are closed Thursday for Thanksgiving and close early, at 1 p.m. Eastern, on Friday. Wednesday brings a pile of government reports, including a revised estimate of third-quarter economic growth, jobless claims and October’s PCE inflation index, the Federal Reserve’s preferred price gauge. Companies reporting results include Hewlett Packard Enterprise on Monday, Dell, HP, Best Buy and Dollar Tree on Tuesday, and Deere, the farm-equipment maker, on Wednesday.</p></div>')
body.append('<h3>What analysts are saying</h3>')
body.append('<ul class="picks"><li><b>Analysts leaned toward buying: UNH</b> (UnitedHealth Group, the nation’s largest health insurer, which also owns the Optum doctor-group, pharmacy-benefit and data businesses). TD Cowen upgraded it to buy from hold on Friday after a months-long slide in the shares, naming it a top pick for 2027 and saying it sees “a credible path toward mid-high-teens EPS growth through 2028.” The same firm also upgraded rival Humana. Risk: medical costs for Medicare Advantage patients have run hot, and if they stay high the earnings rebound could slip.</li><li><b>Analysts leaned toward buying: PDD</b> (PDD Holdings, the Chinese online retailer behind the Pinduoduo shopping app in China and the Temu discount site in the U.S. and elsewhere). Macquarie raised it to outperform on Friday, saying “Temu showed clearer signs of approaching profitability” and calling the company’s marketing spending prudent; BNP Paribas, separately, lifted the stock to neutral from underperform. Risk: the main business in China faces a weak consumer economy, and Temu remains exposed to U.S. tariff and trade rules.</li><li><b>Analysts leaned toward buying: OKLO</b> (Oklo, a California company developing small nuclear reactors to sell electricity, including to data centers, though none is yet running). Guggenheim upgraded it to buy from neutral with a $38 target, saying it was encouraged by progress toward commercial operations in power, medical isotopes and fuel, and suggesting the recent selloff as an entry point. Risk: Oklo has no operating reactor and little revenue, so delays in federal licensing or construction could hit the shares hard.</li><li><b>Analysts turned cautious: GWW</b> (W.W. Grainger, a large distributor of maintenance, repair and safety supplies to businesses). Baird’s David Manthey cut it to neutral from outperform on Friday and lowered his target to $1,500 from $1,600, and did the same to rival Fastenal, saying the shares already trade near peak valuations while profit margins are near their highs and price increases are slowing. He also warned that a slowdown in AI spending or the wider economy could make earnings forecasts look too high. Grainger fell about 3.5 percent. Risk to the cautious view: Baird still expects the longer-term industrial recovery to continue, which could keep demand strong.</li><li><b>Analysts turned cautious: BILL</b> (BILL Holdings, which sells software that small and midsize businesses use to pay bills and send invoices). At least seven firms, including JPMorgan, Jefferies, UBS and TD Cowen, cut it to hold or neutral after the company agreed Thursday to be bought by the investment firm TPG and payments company Corpay for $63.25 a share in cash, about $6 billion; several called the price disappointing but expect shareholders to approve. With a fixed cash offer, the stock has little room to rise. Risk: if the deal falls through before its expected closing in the first half of 2027, the shares could drop well below the offer price.</li></ul>')
body.append('<p class="note">This is information about what analysts said, not advice to buy or sell.</p>')
body.append('<h3>Commodities</h3>')
