body.append('<p class="note">Oil bounced on Friday after a tanker fire near the Strait of Hormuz, the narrow Persian Gulf shipping lane, revived supply worries, but both benchmarks fell for a third straight week (WTI about 5 percent, Brent about 3 percent); the December WTI contract expired Friday, and one farm wire listed it at $84.09, up 88 cents, while the exchange settlement reported by energy news services is used here. Soybeans gained on fund buying and export demand for soybean meal, while corn slipped as harvest nears its end. Arkansas gas fell 18 cents in a week ahead of Thanksgiving travel, and AAA said the national average could drop about 15 cents more by Thursday. Grains, livestock, rice and cotton: <i>(single source: Brownfield Ag News)</i>.</p>')
body.append('<h2>On the Field</h2>')
body.append(P('<b>Arkansas State football</b> (4-6, 2-4 Sun Belt) plays its first game under interim coach Jalen Ortiz tonight at Louisiana Tech, 6 p.m. Central at Joe Aillet Stadium in Ruston, La., streaming on ESPN+. Ortiz, the associate head coach and cornerbacks coach, took over after the university fired Butch Jones this week; Jones went 30-43 in six seasons and took the Red Wolves to bowl games in each of the last three. The stakes are plain: a loss ends any hope of a bowl, and even a win leaves A-State needing to beat Troy at home next week to reach six victories. Louisiana Tech (8-2, 6-0), coached by Sonny Cumbie, has won seven straight, has already clinched the Sun Belt West and is about a two-touchdown favorite in its final home game. Watch the turnover battle: Tech quarterback Blake Baker has thrown 17 touchdown passes and only one interception, and the Bulldogs have forced 26 turnovers, five of them last week, while A-State’s Trey Owens has 2,350 passing yards, 12 touchdowns and nine interceptions.||The coaching search continues with the help of the firm TurnkeyZRG. Hugh Freeze, who led A-State to a 10-2 record and a Sun Belt title in 2011 before coaching at Ole Miss and Auburn, has been reported by RedWolfReport and others to top the school’s list, but no offer or hire had been announced as of Saturday morning.'))
body.append(P('<b>Arkansas State men’s basketball</b> (3-2) has the weekend off before a three-game trip to Miami for an event hosted by Florida International. The Red Wolves open against Southern Utah on Monday at 2:30 p.m. Central, then play Long Beach State on Tuesday and FIU on Wednesday, all on the EAB Red Wolves Sports Network. They come in off Wednesday’s 100-69 home win over Arkansas-Pine Bluff and return to First National Bank Arena against Rice on Sunday, Nov. 29.'))
body.append(P('<b>Arkansas Razorbacks football</b> (4-6, 2-5 SEC) plays at No. 5 Texas (8-2, 5-2) this afternoon at 2:30 p.m. Central at Darrell K Royal-Texas Memorial Stadium in Austin, on ESPN. The Hogs, in their first season under coach Ryan Silverfield, are trying to follow last week’s 34-6 upset of South Carolina by playing spoiler against a Texas team still chasing a playoff spot; the Longhorns are favored by roughly three touchdowns. Texas will be without running back Hollywood Smothers and four other players, with pass rusher Colin Simmons listed as questionable. Arkansas is missing defensive back Braydon Lee, tight ends Maddox Lassiter and Gavin Garretson and lineman Bryant Williams. Texas leads the series 58-23.'))
body.append(P('<b>Tennessee Vols football</b> (8-2, 5-2 SEC), No. 9 in the College Football Playoff rankings, plays LSU (6-4, 3-4) on Senior Day at Neyland Stadium in Knoxville at 2:30 p.m. Central, on ABC. It is the Vols’ last home game and an important one for their playoff hopes. LSU is coached by Lane Kiffin, the former Vols coach, in his first season in Baton Rouge; the Tigers lost 22-21 to Texas last week, and oddsmakers make the game close to a toss-up. Quarterback George MacIntyre, hurt in the right knee last week at Texas A&amp;M, carried no injury designation on the SEC report and is expected to play, though some reports say the starter will be settled at game time, with freshman Faizon Brandon the alternative. Running back DeSean Bishop, with 1,163 yards and 13 touchdowns, has topped 97 yards in six straight games.'))
body.append('<h2>Afield &amp; Afloat</h2>')
body.append('<p class="note">Ratings are rule-of-thumb estimates from the forecast, the rut calendar and the sun, not measurements of animal or fish behavior.</p>')
body.append('<h3>Deer outlook: Good</h3>')
body.append(P('The rut is still within its peak window for north Arkansas, the dawn is calm, under 5 miles an hour, and a cool start near 46 degrees comes with the barometer sliding ahead of Sunday’s front, a combination that tends to keep deer on their feet early. The catch is the heat: an afternoon near 74 will send most deer to shade by late morning. Best windows: from about 6:15 a.m. to roughly two hours after sunrise, and from 2:51 p.m. until sunset at 4:51; in the rut, 10 a.m. to 2 p.m. is realistic for bucks cruising between doe groups.||<i>Today’s tip:</i> with a light southerly breeze, hunt stands on the south side of bedding cover so your scent drifts away from it, and give the morning sit everything you have; on a day this warm, a buck seeking does is far more likely to be moving at 8 a.m. than at 4 p.m.'))
body.append(P('<b>Tomorrow, Sunday (estimate: Fair).</b> Gray skies and a mild dawn near 51, with a 64 percent chance of rain over the day and wind rising toward 15 miles an hour by evening as the front comes through. The rut still helps, but the warm start and the wind trim the odds. Windows: about 6:16 a.m. to two hours after sunrise, and 2:51 p.m. to sunset at 4:51, with midday worth a sit between showers.||<i>Tomorrow’s tip:</i> if the rain holds off at dawn, be in the stand early, because deer often feed hard just before a front; once the wind picks up, move to a sheltered bottom or the downwind edge of a thicket, where deer go to escape it. It is the last day of modern gun in Zone 4, so plan accordingly.'))
body.append('<h3>Fishing outlook: Fair</h3>')
body.append(P('A warm, sunny afternoon near 74 and a light chop are in the fisherman’s favor on Craighead Forest Lake and Lake Frierson, but the barometer is falling fairly quickly ahead of the front, which can make fish moody. Best times: late morning through mid-afternoon as the shallows warm, and the last hour before sunset at 4:51.||<i>Today’s tip:</i> the sun will warm the shallow, dark-bottomed coves and the north banks that face it; work a slow crankbait or a shaky-head worm along those banks for bass, and look for crappie around brush piles in about 8 to 12 feet of water on small minnows or a 1/16-ounce jig.'))
body.append(P('<b>Tomorrow, Sunday (estimate: Fair).</b> Overcast and mild, an afternoon near 64, with rain chances and a wind that stiffens late in the day. Best times: late morning to mid-afternoon, before the wind builds, plus any lull near sunset.||<i>Tomorrow’s tip:</i> clouds and a light rain can draw bass shallow and make them less wary, so try a spinnerbait along wind-blown points and the edges of cover; once the rain sets in or the wind passes 15 miles an hour, catfish on cut bait along the dam or the deepest channel are the safer bet.'))
body.append('<h3>In season now, and what is next (zones 3, 4, 6 and 8)</h3>')
body.append('<table class="mk"><tr><th>Zone</th><th>Archery</th><th>Youth gun</th><th>Modern gun</th><th>Muzzleloader</th></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Sept. 26, 2026 – Feb. 28, 2027</td><td>Nov. 7-8, 2026; Jan. 2-3, 2027</td><td>Nov. 14 – Dec. 6; Dec. 26-28, 2026</td><td>Oct. 17-25; Dec. 12-14, 2026</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Sept. 26, 2026 – Feb. 28, 2027</td><td>Nov. 7-8, 2026; Jan. 2-3, 2027</td><td>Nov. 14-22; Dec. 26-28, 2026</td><td>Closed</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Sept. 26, 2026 – Feb. 28, 2027</td><td>Nov. 7-8, 2026; Jan. 2-3, 2027</td><td>Nov. 14 – Dec. 6; Dec. 26-28, 2026</td><td>Oct. 17-25; Dec. 12-14, 2026</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Sept. 26, 2026 – Feb. 28, 2027</td><td>Nov. 7-8, 2026; Jan. 2-3, 2027</td><td>Nov. 14 – Dec. 6; Dec. 26-28, 2026</td><td>Oct. 17-25; Dec. 12-14, 2026</td></tr></table>')
body.append(P('<b>Deer: Zone 4’s last gun weekend.</b> Modern gun season in Zone 4, which includes Craighead County, ends Sunday, Nov. 22; after that, Zone 4 hunters have archery through Feb. 28 and the Dec. 26-28 holiday gun hunt, and Zone 4 has no muzzleloader (Alternative Firearms) season. In Zones 3, 6 and 8, modern gun runs through Sunday, Dec. 6, followed by the Dec. 12-14 Alternative Firearms weekend and the same Dec. 26-28 holiday hunt. Each zone’s limit is four deer, no more than two bucks; in Zone 4 only one of the antlerless deer may be taken with a modern gun, while Zones 3, 6 and 8 allow three antlerless by gun. Craighead, Jackson, Mississippi and Randolph counties fall under the Game and Fish Commission’s chronic wasting disease (CWD) rules, the measures meant to slow a fatal deer disease; inside the CWD Management Zone there is no antler restriction, while elsewhere in Zones 3, 4 and 8 a buck must have at least three points on one side. Dogs are not allowed for deer in Zones 3 and 4; in Zones 6 and 8 they are allowed through Dec. 6, and nonresidents may use them only Nov. 28 to Dec. 6.'))
body.append(P('<b>Ducks open today.</b> The first segment of duck season runs from this morning through Sunday, Nov. 29, then closes until the second segment, Dec. 10-23, and the third, Dec. 26 through Jan. 31. The daily limit is six ducks, including no more than four mallards (two of them hens), three wood ducks, three pintails, two redheads, two canvasbacks and one scaup; coots are 15 a day. Geese are open in the same segments, with daily limits of two Canadas, two white-fronted and 20 light geese. On wildlife management areas, shooting stops at noon and hunters must be off flooded areas by 1 p.m. (by noon at Dave Donaldson Black River and Bayou Meto). Nonresidents may not hunt waterfowl at Dave Donaldson Black River WMA during this first segment. After 14 months of drought, the Commission expects fewer ducks and harder hunting and urges hunters to check water levels for their spot at agfc.com/wetlandmanagement before they go.'))
body.append(P('<b>Small game.</b> Quail season is open statewide through Feb. 7 with a daily limit of six; some WMAs set shorter dates, so check the area’s page. AGFC listings for areas such as Big Lake WMA show squirrel (daily limit 12) and rabbit (daily limit eight) open through Feb. 28. The cooler weather coming this week usually makes for better rabbit and squirrel hunting, once the leaves are down and the woods open up.'))
body.append('<h2>Postscript</h2>')
body.append('<p class="ps">My sorrow, when she’s here with me,<br>Thinks these dark days of autumn rain<br>Are beautiful as days can be;<br>She loves the bare, the withered tree;<br>She walks the sodden pasture lane.<br><br>Her pleasure will not let me stay.<br>She talks and I am fain to list:<br>She’s glad the birds are gone away,<br>She’s glad her simple worsted grey<br>Is silver now with clinging mist.<br><br>The desolate, deserted trees,<br>The faded earth, the heavy sky,<br>The beauties she so truly sees,<br>She thinks I have no eye for these,<br>And vexes me for reason why.<br><br>Not yesterday I learned to know<br>The love of bare November days<br>Before the coming of the snow,<br>But it were vain to tell her so,<br>And they are better for her praise.</p>')
body.append('<p class="ps">&mdash; Robert Frost, “My November Guest,” from <i>A Boy’s Will</i> (1913). With the last summery day giving way to Sunday’s rain and a gray, chilly Thanksgiving week, Frost’s young poem is a reminder that the bare, gray end of November has a beauty of its own.</p>')
body.append('<p class="sign">Until tomorrow, with good wishes &mdash;<br><b>The Editor</b></p>')
body.append('<div class="foot">Compiled from many reports.</div>')

# Pastimes
import os, json as _json
fleet = _json.load(open('fleet.json')) if os.path.exists('fleet.json') else None
def fleet_svg(d):
    n=d['size']; cs=30; lab=18; W=lab+n*cs; H=lab+n*cs
    o=[f'<svg viewBox="0 0 {W} {H}" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" style="width:100%;height:auto;display:block;font-family:inherit">']
    for c in range(n): o.append(f'<text x="{lab+c*cs+cs/2}" y="12" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10" fill="#8a6a35">{chr(65+c)}</text>')
    for r in range(n):
        o.append(f'<text x="{lab/2}" y="{lab+r*cs+cs/2+4}" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10" fill="#4a3618">{r+1}</text>')
        for c in range(n):
            x=lab+c*cs;y=lab+r*cs
            o.append(f'<rect x="{x}" y="{y}" width="{cs}" height="{cs}" fill="none" stroke="#8a6a35" stroke-width="1"/><text x="{x+cs/2}" y="{y+cs/2+6}" text-anchor="middle" font-size="17" font-weight="bold" fill="#2b2118">{d["grid"][r][c]}</text>')
    o.append('</svg>'); return ''.join(o)
body.append('<div class="page pastimes"><h2>The Pastimes</h2>')
left='<div><h3>I. Mate in Two</h3>'+f'<div class="board">{past["chess_svg"]}</div>'+f'<p>{past["chess_prompt"]} White is shown with the lighter, outlined pieces; the board is oriented with White at the bottom.</p></div>'
if fleet:
    tot=sum(len(x['word']) for x in fleet['ships'])
    clues=''.join(f'<li><span class="bl">{" ".join("_" for _ in x["word"])}</span> ({len(x["word"])}) {x["hint"]}</li>' for x in fleet['ships'])
    right=('<div><h3>II. Word Fleet</h3>'+'<div style="width:84%">'+fleet_svg(fleet)+'</div>'+
      f'<ol class="clues fl">{clues}</ol>'
      f'<p class="note">Each clue is a definition. Find its word in the grid, across or down. Fire by putting a pencil dot on a letter: one dot is one shot. Check the answers below, then circle each right letter and cross out each wrong one. Your score is your number of dots. <b>Best possible score: {tot} shots.</b> My shots: ________</p></div>')
    sol_ii='II. '+', '.join(f"{x['word']} {chr(65+x['c'])}{x['r']+1} {'across' if x['dir']=='A' else 'down'}" for x in fleet['ships'])
else:
    right='<div><h3>II. The Four Houses</h3>'+f'<p>{past["logic_intro"]}</p><ol class="clues">'+''.join(f'<li>{c}</li>' for c in past['logic_clues'])+f'</ol><p>{past["logic_question"]}</p></div>'
    sol_ii='II. '+past['logic_answer']
body.append('<div class="two">'+left+right+'</div>')
if os.path.exists('comic/today.svg'):
    body.append('<h3 style="margin-top:2pt;margin-bottom:0">III. Sorry, Earth</h3><div class="comic" style="margin:1pt 0 0 0"><img src="comic/today.png" style="width:92%;display:block;margin:0 auto"></div>')
body.append('<div class="flip box"><b>Solutions printed upside down.</b><br>I. '+past['chess_solution']+'<br>'+sol_ii+'</div></div>')

html = f'<!doctype html><html><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>The Morning Letter</title><style>{css}</style></head><body>'+'\n'.join(body)+'</body></html>'
open('letter.html','w').write(html)
print(len(html))

